    "explanation": "Materially equivalent correct submissions survive: labels match case-insensitively with space/underscore/hyphen separators and optional emphasis, quotes, trailing colon or period; ids match with or without dashes in any case; bill types accept the Excel boolean or TRUE/True/true (FALSE/False/false); integers may be stored as floats (approx_equals on numbers, and values_equal is bool-aware so 1 cannot pass for TRUE). The strict aspects — a number rather than text, no formula, exact row/column placement — are stated verbatim in the instruction. Route M1 in consistency/shortcut_audit.md scores 1.0 while varying title case, float formatting, upper-case ids, dashless order ids, mixed-case boolean text, column widths and frozen panes."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__surface_form_brittleness": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "Every matcher is a full-cell anchored regex (\\A...\\Z with re.search), an exact numeric approx_equals with zero tolerance, an in_array of boolean/word forms, or an integer extent equality — none is a substring or token-whitelist that a wrong answer could satisfy by mimicry: 'billed_practices | 12' or '12 or 13' fail, and a wrong number cannot pass by including the right token. Conversely no semantically correct formatting variant I tested (case, dashes, quotes/emphasis, float storage, boolean-vs-text) is rejected. The matchers check exactly what their descriptions claim."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__verifier_execution_completeness": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "tests/test.sh runs tests/test_gvs.sh, which execs /app/tests/test_outputs.py; with agentic_llm_as_judge allowed, _emit_engine_owned_reward runs every manifest verifier_config through VerifierEngine. The oracle run confirms all 8 executed with 0 unscored. Failures inside rl_world_verifiers are classified (TypeError/FileNotFoundError etc. become per-leaf failures via is_agent_output_failure; infrastructure errors raise and refuse to emit a score rather than silently zeroing)."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__score_bucket_activation": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "The active bucket is the engine's verifier_configs list, and all 8 items carry non-zero weight and did receive evidence in the oracle run. The inert template buckets are honestly inert: state weight 0 with an empty expected_diff.json, sql_verifiers [], trajectory weight 0, and none of them is in allowed_verifier_types. rubric.toml has no scored items, but no requirement is claimed to be covered by it."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__aggregation_normalization": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "flat_verifier_scoring=true pools every item by weight: total weight 7*1.0 + 0.5 = 7.5. I recomputed the documented route rewards and they match exactly — 3 summary configs failing gives 4.5/7.5 = 0.6 (R1), 5 failing gives 2.5/7.5 = 0.3333 (R2), 2 failing gives 5.5/7.5 = 0.7333 (R3/R6/R7/R9), 1 failing gives 6.5/7.5 = 0.8667 (R4), all failing gives 0.0 (R5). The axis weights block in manifest.scoring is vestigial in this path but is echoed only into diagnostics, not into the emitted reward."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__scoring_proportionality": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "The five substantive figures, the 37-row reconciliation log and the layout bound each carry 1.0, and the process floor carries only 0.5 — the smallest weight goes to the least substantive check. A fatal omission is not masked: missing the raw export fails 5 of 8 configs (0.3333), while the cosmetic-but-disclosed layout requirement alone caps a run at 0.7333 rather than dominating. No cosmetic issue can dominate reward and no fatal error is hidden by partial credit."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__check_independence": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "Each config grades a distinct deliverable fact: five separate Summary figures, the per-row Billing log, the extent bounds, and the work floor. Overlap is limited and disclosed in the why_justifications (e.g. missing the raw export moves both the Summary counts and the row log) and reflects genuinely separate requirements — the instruction asks for both the log and the counts, so a defect affecting both is a defect in both deliverables."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__counterfactual_strength": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "consistency/shortcut_audit.md and mutations.json record positive (gold, and a cosmetically different shortest path, both 1.0), negative (row-grain misreading 0.6, exclusive-bill-type reading 0.7333, title-wording entity 0.6, empty-statements-as-silent 0.8667), incomplete (raw export skipped 0.3333, listing-only 0.0, Johnson May missed 0.7333) and adversarial (trashed export included 0.7333, header row 0.7333, extra rows on Billing/Summary caught by the bound, every figure off by one caught) cases. I verified the arithmetic of each and the mechanism of the key ones against the assertions, so the verifier demonstrably separates a genuine solution from plausible defective ones."
  },
  "layer5_verifier_fairness_static__process_grading_justification": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "Reward is overwhelmingly the requested outcome (7.0 of 7.5 weight on the workbook's contents). The single process element, minimum_tool_calls=24 with expected_tools empty, prescribes no particular tool, ordering or narration and sits below the measured 25-call minimum path, so it functions as a fabrication floor (it is what fails the listing-only route) rather than as a workflow mandate. Harness tools are excluded from the count, which is the only way a correct-but-CLI-driven run could be penalised; this is a narrow residual risk given the system prompt directs the agent to the gym's tools."
